    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The argument for overturning Chevron deference is that it allows unelected bureaucrats to override clear statutory language, which is an unconstitutional delegation of legislative power. Conversely, proponents argue that deference is necessary to allow expert agencies to implement complex regulatory schemes effectively. The concerns of those wanting it overturned are valid because they rightly point to the erosion of democratic accountability in government. However, the concerns of those wanting to keep it are also valid, as they highlight the practical need for agencies to possess technical expertise.",
